data: {"choices":[{"delta":{"reasoning_content":"No browser automation libraries installed. I can install puppeteer-core, but that may be heavy. Alternatively, I can use curl to fetch the page and examine the HTML plus JS bundles. But the data-component attributes will be rendered by React at runtime. However, the source code of @codesweep-ai/ui components will contain `data-component=\"Header\"` etc. in the JS bundle. So I can grep the bundle.\n\nBut for the greeting color and button functionality, I can verify by:\n1. Checking App.jsx source code directly - it has the correct logic.\n2. Checking the rendered HTML won't show the dynamic content without JS execution.\n\nBetter approach: Use the installed chromium-browser directly via a small shell script or use Node with a headless browser. I can install `puppeteer-core` quickly. Let me try installing it globally or locally in the project? The instructions say don't modify code, but installing a dev dependency for verification is okay... Actually, I should avoid modifying package.json if possible. I can install it in a temp directory or use npx.\n\nWait, I can use `npx puppeteer` but that would install. Better: use `npx playwright-core`? Let me just use Python with requests and BeautifulSoup? No, that won't execute JS.\n\nI can use the browser directly. Let me use `chromium-browser --headless --dump-dom` to get the rendered DOM. Yes! Chromium has a `--dump-dom` flag that dumps the DOM after executing JS.\n\nSo the plan:\n1. Start `npm run serve` in background on port 5173.\n2. Wait a moment.\n3. Use `chromium-browser --headless --no-sandbox --disable-gpu --dump-dom http://localhost:5173/` to get rendered DOM.\n4. Check the DOM for required elements.\n5. Also curl the page and check headers/status.\n6. Check package.json dependency.\n\nLet me start the server and run the headless browser."},"finish_reason":null,"index":0,"raw_output":null}],"created":1789849396,"id":"chatcmpl-39a47a1f052641fea1c0738365e799ff","model":"accounts/fireworks/models/kimi-k2p7-code","object":"chat.completion.chunk","usage":null}

